WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.175
PQC for TLS and web services prepares web communication for a future where some current

00:00:05.175 --> 00:00:08.235
public-key algorithms may no longer provide enough protection.

00:00:08.235 --> 00:00:12.880
TLS protects browsers, APIs, service-to-service calls, mobile backends, gateways,

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:15.380
SaaS platforms, and internal applications.

00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:18.120
The migration challenge is not simply choosing an algorithm.

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:22.340
It is understanding how handshake roles, certificates, clients, libraries, proxies,

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:23.920
and operations fit together.

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:27.155
The most important technical distinction is between cryptographic roles.

00:00:27.155 --> 00:00:30.395
Key establishment helps create shared key material for the session.

00:00:30.395 --> 00:00:33.920
Digital signatures help authenticate identities and verify integrity.

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:37.230
Symmetric encryption protects the application data after the handshake.

00:00:37.230 --> 00:00:41.460
Post-quantum KEMs, such as ML-KEM conceptually, relate to key establishment.

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:45.700
Post-quantum signature algorithms, such as ML-DSA and SLH-DSA conceptually,

00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:48.600
relate to authentication and signing use cases.

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:52.350
Hybrid key exchange can help during the transition, but it should be standards-based,

00:00:52.350 --> 00:00:53.995
tested, monitored, and governed.

00:00:53.995 --> 00:00:58.310
It does not remove the need for compatibility work or downgrade awareness.

00:00:58.310 --> 00:01:02.095
Certificate and PKI migration is its own ecosystem challenge involving browsers,

00:01:02.095 --> 00:01:06.005
operating systems, certificate authorities, trust stores, libraries, devices,

00:01:06.005 --> 00:01:07.320
and enterprise products.

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:10.920
Web trust changes only safely when the whole validation path is ready.

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:13.880
Strong readiness starts with inventory and prioritization.

00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:17.735
Find TLS endpoints, classify data, identify owners, map clients,

00:01:17.735 --> 00:01:22.470
track libraries and vendors, test in labs, deploy in stages, monitor production signals,

00:01:22.470 --> 00:01:24.000
and prepare rollback plans.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:25.940
The goal is not panic-driven replacement.

00:01:25.940 --> 00:01:28.920
The goal is practical crypto agility for critical web services,

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:32.225
so organizations can adapt deliberately as standards, libraries,

00:01:32.225 --> 00:01:33.560
and vendor support mature.
